#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166705 :question/id #uuid "e8353dba-48d7-5f4f-8ba5-97e4e22b6e44" :question/math-academy-id "e-9050" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis where $x>0$. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\, \\rm{ m/s}$ and the magnitude of its acceleration is $\\sin^2 x \\,\\rm{m/s}^2.$ Given that the acceleration is directed toward $O,$ what differential equation describes this motion?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/f1/f187eff194f136838b67ab12b44f8f6882eafb59885a5c3f440d41492def4a42.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $\\sin^2 x$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t} = -\\sin^2 x.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule for differentiation, we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-{sin}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\nv \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x} = -\\sin^2 x.\\qquad \\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nFinally, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}(v^2) = 2v\\dfrac{\\textrm d v}{\\textrm d x}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}\\left(\\dfrac12v^2\\right) = v\\dfrac{\\textrm d v}{\\textrm d x},\n$$\n\nwe can write our differential equation $(\\ast)$ as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}\\left(\\dfrac12v^2\\right) = -\\sin^2 x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166706 :question/id #uuid "49e02115-6211-564b-8221-d5835a98a064" :question/math-academy-id "e-9051" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x,$ away from a fixed origin $O.$ When the displacement of $P$ from $O$ is $x \\,\\rm{m},$ its velocity is $v\\,\\rm{m/s}$ and its acceleration is $(2x + e^{x-2}) \\, \\rm{m/s}^2.$ If $v = 4 \\, \\rm{m/s}$ when $x = 2 \\, \\textrm m,$ find the velocity of $P$ when $x = 1 \\, \\textrm m.$ Round your answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "![](images/4f/4fdd70a4ca80965c0adce9c60be36b0712852b662cff69f9733f50c739fd1b32.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(2x + e^{x-2}) \\,\\rm{m/s}^2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t} = 2x + e^{x-2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t}= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2\\right)\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2\\right) = 2x + e^{x-2}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2x+{e}^{x-2})\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2 = x^2 + e^{x-2} + C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v = 4 \\, \\rm{m/s}$ when $x = 2 \\, \\textrm m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} & ={2}^{2}+{e}^{2-2}+C \\\\ 8 & =4+1+C \\\\ C & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+{e}^{x-2}+3 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =2({x}^{2}+{e}^{x-2}+3) \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{2({x}^{2}+{e}^{x-2}+3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x) \\gt 0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv = \\sqrt{2\\left(x^2 + e^{x-2} + 3\\right)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity of $P$ at $x = 1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\sqrt{2({1}^{2}+{e}^{1-2}+3)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2(4+{e}^{-1})} \\\\  & ≈2.96\\,\\text{m/s},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166715 :question/id #uuid "80b653d0-5dff-5621-86ee-e1fd336ad740" :question/math-academy-id "e-9455" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x\\geq 0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,\\textrm m,$ the magnitude of its acceleration is $\\dfrac{2}{(x+1)^2}\\, \\rm{m/s}^2$ and is directed toward $O.$ Initially, $P$ is at the origin $O$ and has velocity $v=2\\,\\rm{m/s}.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/ec/eca36626e6a0cfb30d293e684483c37ddce6f18d4004c3f77258e9663565d8d7.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $\\dfrac{2}{(x+1)^2}\\, \\rm{m/s}^2$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t} =-\\dfrac{2}{(x+1)^2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t}= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2\\right)\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2\\right) = -\\dfrac{2}{(x+1)^2}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -\\frac{2}{(x+1)^{2}}\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{2}{x+1}+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=2\\,\\rm{m/s}$ when $x=0\\, \\textrm{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $K,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{2}{x+1}+K \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(2)^{2} & =\\frac{2}{0+1}+K \\\\ 2 & =2+K \\\\ K & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{2}{x+1} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =\\frac{4}{x+1} \\\\ v & =\\pm \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x) > 0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x) = \\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{x+1}}.\n$$\n\nNow, we know that $v= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} = \\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{x+1}}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x+1}} \\\\ \\sqrt{x+1}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2 \\\\ \\int \\sqrt{x+1}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int 2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\sqrt{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}(x+1)^{3/2} & =2t+{C}_{1} \\\\ (x+1)^{3/2} & =3t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C = \\dfrac{3C_1}{2}.$ \n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=1,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\n(x+1)^{3/2} =3t+1.\n$$\n\nFinally, writing $x$ in terms of $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{3/2} & =3t+1 \\\\ x+1 & =(3t+1)^{2/3} \\\\ x & =(3t+1)^{2/3}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167067 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "956a2a68-a066-5d9b-99c4-1ffdab50a6c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}v^{2}\\frac{dv}{dx} = 4x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163481} {:answer/id #uuid "f06249ca-353d-5c5c-aac0-700c2abf2c77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v^{2}\\frac{dv}{dx} = 4x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163482} {:answer/id #uuid "187ad1a9-4758-51ce-bc58-282e7ecd3b91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v\\frac{dv}{dx} = 4x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163483} {:answer/id #uuid "413bb140-de62-5a16-9c14-2aea7a9922d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{v}\\frac{dv}{dx} = 4x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163484} {:answer/id #uuid "6ff73680-3743-5d57-90d1-3587578c0f5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dx} = 4x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163485}] :answer-field/id #uuid "39280676-c935-5a69-9075-d31a70d69db8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163480} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "421852f4-4f42-5b1c-bf9e-1abfb03a3d74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{v}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=4{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375709} {:answer/id #uuid "f5ad6f5f-5a30-5e2d-bf32-8da8b862cd80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=4{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375710} {:answer/id #uuid "ebe84925-988d-521e-8297-13f2aa128cd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=4{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375711} {:answer/id #uuid "2dc293e9-bbda-5d87-8b09-2a737ba5a341" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=4{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375712} {:answer/id #uuid "7667ef6e-cc23-5c48-9b96-ca442081c5c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=4{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375713}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7667ef6e-cc23-5c48-9b96-ca442081c5c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=4{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375713} :answer-field/id #uuid "58d8638f-e495-5f20-b9d1-2a666c70bb7b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375708}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f1d3a897-4665-5a3c-93ac-66ae33ab4d78" :question/math-academy-id "q-107998" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is $4{x}^{2}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ Which differential equation describes this motion?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/88/888a2f1f537f7381379a6f85dc850f29b42193eb53938832be8ede47fbe8264a.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $4{x}^{2}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=4{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule for differentiation, we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=4{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we obtain the differential equation\n\n$$\nv\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=4{x}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167068 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0efc57f9-3b68-505a-890f-a6fd6cf1e109" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241307} {:answer/id #uuid "19cd3def-04bb-529a-b9f2-db87808c05c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241401} {:answer/id #uuid "38da12bd-ce5e-5d20-9221-a12ebc51f6c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=2{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241690} {:answer/id #uuid "3b5bb1c2-0cb4-50aa-a486-98783cd32800" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)={x}^{2}+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241709} {:answer/id #uuid "cbee714a-7515-5c91-aef2-65b895acf095" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=\\frac{8}{5}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242972}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19cd3def-04bb-529a-b9f2-db87808c05c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241401} :answer-field/id #uuid "920a508d-c801-5105-8257-5f6f6e4caa7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243713}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cfc6326b-3536-5d27-a880-335a706849db" :question/math-academy-id "q-107999" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is $4x\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $P$ has a displacement of $2\\,m$ when its velocity is $8\\,m/s,$ find $v$ in terms of $x.$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "![](images/01/017463f3ac8abac9c2b00c4d9a715a4109a5f4bc1b070e0a12c4d3a7bac64351.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $4x\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=4x.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=4x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x=\\int 4x\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=2{x}^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $x=2\\,\\text{m}$ when $v=8\\,\\text{m/s},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =2{x}^{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(8)^{2} & =2(2)^{2}+C \\\\ C & =32-8 \\\\ C & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =2{x}^{2}+24 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =4{x}^{2}+48 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =4({x}^{2}+12) \\\\ v & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+12}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167069 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4448af6-7baf-5a5b-b8d1-39a077ebf8f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.1m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163493} {:answer/id #uuid "c9857de1-4db2-5ad9-a25d-dde1b667afaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.7m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163494} {:answer/id #uuid "f1bea300-34aa-57f5-afe8-1a6e6ecf1c64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.2m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163495} {:answer/id #uuid "2f61cdd9-b058-5af6-b23f-8b8773806d8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.8m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163496} {:answer/id #uuid "4113fec4-4e93-5ab7-8a45-bc6f10d75a97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.4m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163497}] :answer-field/id #uuid "086d497a-6cd2-50e5-a303-5b7f3ca50234" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163492} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "371f7e20-4de5-5368-967d-dcf19dbb8e9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.8\\text{ m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378390} {:answer/id #uuid "d0bc1409-3a05-5e3b-9fa9-a5a38be59893" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.1\\text{ m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378391} {:answer/id #uuid "aa0f5588-3cc0-5c85-9c0a-25cb410efa25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.4\\text{ m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378392} {:answer/id #uuid "45996835-9efe-5091-8f06-bb6a21de769b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.7\\text{ m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378393} {:answer/id #uuid "24b767f3-5837-5969-ac0d-79fcacc77a0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.2\\text{ m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378394}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45996835-9efe-5091-8f06-bb6a21de769b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.7\\text{ m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378393} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e008b2d-cd2e-5642-b34a-0d82523099d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378389}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9c92f4b3-2f0f-5819-981c-8fa3d450f8a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-108002" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x,$ away from a fixed origin $O.$ When the displacement of $P$ from $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is $(2+3{e}^{-x})\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $v=2\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,\\text{m},$ find the velocity of $P$ when $x=3\\,\\text{m}.$ Round your answer to one decimal place." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "![](images/ec/ec4af1357e75dcee6d7d5412467c2ee2ea9e54fa38fb6e83817b0ae909c529aa.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(2+3{e}^{-x})\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=2+3{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=2+3{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2+3{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=2x-3{e}^{-x}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=2\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $x=0\\,\\text{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(2)^{2} & =2(0)-3{e}^{-0}+C \\\\ 2 & =-3+C \\\\ C & =2+3 \\\\ C & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =2x-3{e}^{-x}+5 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =4x-6{e}^{-x}+10 \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{4x-6{e}^{-x}+10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv=\\sqrt{4x-6{e}^{-x}+10}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity of $P$ at $x=3$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\sqrt{4(3)-6{e}^{-3}+10} \\\\ v & =\\sqrt{22-6{e}^{-3}} \\\\ v & ≈4.7\\,\\text{m/s},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167070 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e35e36b9-a107-5bbb-b7c8-41e8c6dc4879" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = \\ln (2t + 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163499} {:answer/id #uuid "0f73d228-c446-5a10-bd78-6f0c0ce9ebfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = 2\\ln (2t + 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163500} {:answer/id #uuid "1027b420-bf83-5663-94f1-7b7cba8947e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = 2(e^{t} - 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163501} {:answer/id #uuid "286d3701-b83d-5411-b8b4-d61e90e0c09d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = e^{2t} - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163502} {:answer/id #uuid "2dd8b19a-43df-534a-a890-b4b4c79efd58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = 2\\ln (t + 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163503}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ec077a5b-2139-5072-b8e7-16039344858f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163498} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e533c757-28f8-54c9-936f-065a840dfb4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378396} {:answer/id #uuid "4e69bb27-3048-5959-86d5-d0da591b3305" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={e}^{2t}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378397} {:answer/id #uuid "055a79db-f0b6-57ba-8a99-27bc8639f63e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378398} {:answer/id #uuid "3db5efd1-f875-5b3c-940e-776606820653" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378399} {:answer/id #uuid "40ca2a73-1923-5806-ac17-e9f3ffd63f18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=2({e}^{t}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378400}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "055a79db-f0b6-57ba-8a99-27bc8639f63e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378398} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9b1e9a7-214e-5155-8df6-65833d4696d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378395}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e0198649-750b-56f9-a8cb-2fa205e1f49e" :question/math-academy-id "q-108004" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,\\text{m},$ the magnitude of its acceleration is $2{e}^{-x}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed toward $O.$ Initially, $P$ is at the origin $O$ and has velocity $v=2\\,m/s.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/ad/ad437638f22672a10a183f1521929637f8d43725f37a9736c569516232e7c468.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $2{e}^{-x}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-2{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-2{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -2{e}^{-x}\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =2{e}^{-x}+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $x=0\\,\\text{m}$ when $v=2\\,\\text{m/s},$ we can find the value of the constant $K,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =2{e}^{-x}+K \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(2)^{2} & =2{e}^{0}+K \\\\ K & =2-2 \\\\ K & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =2{e}^{-x} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =4{e}^{-x} \\\\ v & =\\pm 2{e}^{-x/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=2{e}^{-x/2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=2{e}^{-x/2}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2{e}^{-x/2} \\\\ {e}^{x/2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2 \\\\ \\int {e}^{x/2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int 2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int {e}^{x/2}\\text{d}x & =\\int 2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ 2{e}^{x/2} & =2t+{C}_{1} \\\\ {e}^{x/2} & =t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=\\frac{{C}_{1}}{2}.$ \n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=1,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{e}^{x/2}=t+1.\n$$\n\nFinally, writing $x$ in terms of $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x/2} & =t+1 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x/2}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1) \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1) \\\\ x & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167071 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b6c953d-c2b8-5a7c-a8c7-231519bfef26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=6{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241711} {:answer/id #uuid "83918c12-0cf7-59ca-8bf7-acfdb46d3d5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({v}^{2})=-6{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242331} {:answer/id #uuid "8785a393-91ea-5310-a7f5-13d63af27a09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-6{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242367} {:answer/id #uuid "90a5c916-697d-57a1-948f-0c9b310e9790" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-6{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242446} {:answer/id #uuid "bcc8c5b4-66b6-54be-b66a-62f355fd5fba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=6{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242836}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8785a393-91ea-5310-a7f5-13d63af27a09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-6{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242367} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d3ca676-90e7-517d-ac4f-acc96b4cf09b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243731}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cde0f0e6-e59d-5767-8e42-f1f093f582b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-108010" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis where $x>0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and the magnitude of its acceleration is $6{x}^{3}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ Given that the acceleration is directed toward $O,$ which differential equation describes this motion?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/52/52fc197612dac35373ecebf771b8444505902b5c6e00c7c55d2b21dab4809136.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $6{x}^{3}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-6{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule for differentiation, we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-6{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\nv\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=6{x}^{3}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nFinally, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({v}^{2})=2v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x},\n$$\n\nwe can write our differential equation $(∗)$ as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-6{x}^{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167089 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ebf18ee-af52-58eb-9db4-f851658ecd7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253931} {:answer/id #uuid "1f068fc2-f6b1-5e9e-af2b-751b98bcdc5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253936} {:answer/id #uuid "49f209c9-1626-5df5-aefb-bdf07e8a5715" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{2}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254305} {:answer/id #uuid "de5cdbb3-0dd8-544a-8444-7a6932630c5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=1-{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255642} {:answer/id #uuid "e35e425f-e29f-5937-be75-0ebb5ff34e95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={e}^{t}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255683}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e35e425f-e29f-5937-be75-0ebb5ff34e95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={e}^{t}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255683} :answer-field/id #uuid "877a8eaf-76c0-5b50-a12d-6f6b23536812" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256174}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8488fc53-7024-5b0e-acc8-a7217b9f9e03" :question/math-academy-id "q-122170" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, the velocity is given by $v(x)=(1+x)\\,m/s.$ Initially, $P$ is at the origin $O.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/a8/a8a6f49ead700bee856b1bbdbb10848eac0a57609da14e3967916a36eb56f048.png)\n\nWe're given that the velocity function $v(x)$ is\n\n$$\nv(x)=1+x.\n$$\n\nWe know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=1+x.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1+x \\\\ \\frac{1}{1+x}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1+x}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1+x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|1+x| & =t+K \\\\ 1+x & =C{e}^{t} \\\\ x & =C{e}^{t}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=1,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{t}-1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256598 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e776934-15f3-5d5a-bc85-85702d30bf63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254345} {:answer/id #uuid "8aed0bfe-715b-58b2-83db-0765e4c347fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254898} {:answer/id #uuid "98490759-c3aa-5c11-bb54-4873cf784b5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255017} {:answer/id #uuid "d064b916-01fb-5e7f-91fe-961c9ef8b999" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{v}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255526} {:answer/id #uuid "d1ee7bb1-e061-5086-8ede-9c560677f188" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255542}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e776934-15f3-5d5a-bc85-85702d30bf63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254345} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef9b4f98-7435-59d0-963e-2b72d025372f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256346}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "739ad301-f056-5c4f-be19-03294cd7de14" :question/math-academy-id "q-122305" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\,\\text{m/s}$ and its acceleration is ${e}^{-2x}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ Which differential equation describes this motion?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/dd/dd924518ae28ee8bf010e76b95ddbd68a26137b8b1f28753900455a87e1df799.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is ${e}^{-2x}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule for differentiation, we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we obtain the differential equation\n\n$$\nv\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{-2x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256600 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c5b2eeb-074d-51bf-9b3f-10803e274c41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253770} {:answer/id #uuid "56351265-a52c-5206-885d-fa5dfeb276eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254428} {:answer/id #uuid "afa87942-4e3f-5a55-ae5f-5d22dd94cb53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255214} {:answer/id #uuid "d4b91cfd-f727-572e-865c-a94f7aba262d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255559} {:answer/id #uuid "e6bf1029-4403-54dd-a55a-c9e8275e658a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255702}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6bf1029-4403-54dd-a55a-c9e8275e658a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255702} :answer-field/id #uuid "b673d4fe-7d5a-50cb-99f3-481a645a95f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256249}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a2aa7557-417c-5f06-bca6-b63a94c74ba4" :question/math-academy-id "q-122307" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis where $x>0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and the magnitude of its acceleration is $\\frac{1}{x+1}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ Given that the acceleration is directed toward $O,$ which differential equation describes this motion?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/47/473a3f91d91351499d5849ee07a86cfcfc2b257949d6ebba68b3261201f2bf68.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $\\frac{1}{x+1}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule for differentiation, we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we obtain the differential equation\n\n$$\nv\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{x+1}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256601 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08989d71-d44c-554a-bce5-f49fba344fb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={cos}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253732} {:answer/id #uuid "485c6662-97ec-5254-8bb7-1d15dbc744b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={cos}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254291} {:answer/id #uuid "d23b0337-7b09-5e9a-8cfa-fce6700a9517" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-{cos}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255544} {:answer/id #uuid "eac5aa36-49a3-5648-998f-1f48c9122a73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-{cos}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255726} {:answer/id #uuid "edac6096-b0c5-5959-8d95-58a0e84227a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-{cos}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255747}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d23b0337-7b09-5e9a-8cfa-fce6700a9517" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-{cos}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255544} :answer-field/id #uuid "4cd69269-dfc3-5489-a6f7-c96d7e0ca756" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256068}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "226fe9ff-a963-5e5f-900b-474a261bf3ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-122310" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis where $x>0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is ${cos}^{2}⁡x\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ Given that the acceleration is directed toward $O,$ which differential equation describes this motion?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/76/768db6b8051d8f4bea528f6c5b5d386ffc0d6529517d2fe72641cb33f6e8ac10.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is ${cos}^{2}⁡x$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-{cos}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule for differentiation, we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-{cos}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we obtain the differential equation\n\n$$\nv\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-{cos}^{2}⁡x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256602 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b629cfb-8c18-53b4-b161-169a4b30e42e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({v}^{2})=-{2}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253760} {:answer/id #uuid "62c83a52-5aa0-5f20-8990-378d6242ecbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-{2}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254556} {:answer/id #uuid "95c1f05e-e9f9-5ec0-a592-d641fa3f5e8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-{2}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254989} {:answer/id #uuid "daa45409-cc5a-5cd8-956a-f20e65d4fb1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})={2}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255611} {:answer/id #uuid "f9ccbae0-8402-599d-bbdd-559aba1d4a6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={2}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255858}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95c1f05e-e9f9-5ec0-a592-d641fa3f5e8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-{2}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254989} :answer-field/id #uuid "f811244f-6ed8-541d-bc5b-0c6f81c25239" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256357}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9a2d183e-8156-5091-9a57-168bcd9da833" :question/math-academy-id "q-122311" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis where $x>0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is ${2}^{x}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ Given that the acceleration is directed toward $O,$ which differential equation describes this motion?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/b6/b69783c5b926cb22a6e4321b449d006071917618da601d0ba2bfa109ffde6a0f.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is ${2}^{x}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-{2}^{x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule for differentiation, we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-{2}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\nv\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-{2}^{x}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nFinally, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({v}^{2})=2v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x},\n$$\n\nwe can write our differential equation $(∗)$ as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-{2}^{x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256603 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2da06fcc-39d5-5fd9-9fdf-dd52ccdbebb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254078} {:answer/id #uuid "53e9fb61-38f9-50e7-848b-f4f53f073d5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2t-\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254402} {:answer/id #uuid "b006fd75-7355-56e0-986d-31463204dc8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\pi +t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255219} {:answer/id #uuid "e45f6fc8-f57d-5871-9a4c-9a83794c79ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255691} {:answer/id #uuid "f36dca5a-9486-5470-9b00-1a4d28b97e8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\pi t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255802}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e45f6fc8-f57d-5871-9a4c-9a83794c79ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255691} :answer-field/id #uuid "e344a9bb-3e71-57fd-bc54-29f1efec8291" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256324}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "74ce0e61-7285-5a7d-b7a9-5a53effb65f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-122312" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, the velocity is given by $v(x)=(1+4{x}^{2})\\,m/s.$ Initially, $P$ is at the origin $O.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t$ where $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{8}].$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/59/59c4a51d419b6e42633579b687cde16a2842895c589f818942c10aa304bc98a7.png)\n\nWe're given that the velocity function $v(x)$ is\n\n$$\nv(x)=1+4{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=1+4{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1+4{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1+(2x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡2x & =t+K \\\\ 2x & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2t+C) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2t+C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=0,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡2t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256604 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99f898f6-ca81-58c8-be58-53ec019e222d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255029} {:answer/id #uuid "a17bd3fd-17ec-5c1f-ab16-4d4b74ac4241" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255092} {:answer/id #uuid "cbc7da63-0a0d-5085-8db4-e90d2bb035b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{v}^{2})=-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255479} {:answer/id #uuid "edf2a6d4-69d5-5f9d-b7ef-757523bab7e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255751} {:answer/id #uuid "ff229922-7ee9-51e6-a292-0a4aeea645a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255915}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "edf2a6d4-69d5-5f9d-b7ef-757523bab7e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255751} :answer-field/id #uuid "d17ff7a1-2ba4-5cce-aab0-0c19b7214153" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256297}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7ea0e0b4-75c6-564d-ac9d-36609478cb4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-122313" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis where $x>0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is $\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ Given that the acceleration is directed toward $O,$ which differential equation describes this motion?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/00/00da8cdff7814df7368ff00924360db24a93934c5ae1c87ceb5daa6b00df37a7.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule for differentiation, we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\nv\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nFinally, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({v}^{2})=2v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x},\n$$\n\nwe can write our differential equation $(∗)$ as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256605 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17ad6d9b-511a-5e4e-9f50-a130779e8a8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=3{e}^{2t}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253875} {:answer/id #uuid "411f9dfd-fa6f-5f77-bbd2-3d2ac15069e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=3{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254225} {:answer/id #uuid "61fbb296-5dba-5d27-8375-9e4f53fcebd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=2{e}^{3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254548} {:answer/id #uuid "ad9b03ad-e9a8-567a-880f-0ecd64d5c7dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=2{e}^{3{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255201} {:answer/id #uuid "d0b52ba8-9f87-5b98-99c9-1a660ae93a57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={e}^{3t}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255531}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61fbb296-5dba-5d27-8375-9e4f53fcebd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=2{e}^{3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254548} :answer-field/id #uuid "52415971-fdfd-5729-8b8c-b4bf76d93e45" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256083}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "77f0dbad-663b-5cf7-926d-ed38aebf171d" :question/math-academy-id "q-122314" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, the velocity is given by $v(x)=3x\\,m/s.$ If $x=2\\,m$ when $t=0,$ find $x$ as a function of time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/ef/efdb69673b4088d3c021d78282642ff7abc57158e23704cd3d5e7ff2559cbdbb.png)\n\nWe're given that the velocity function $v(x)$ is\n\n$$\nv(x)=3x.\n$$\n\nWe know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=3x.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =3x \\\\ \\frac{1}{3x}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{3x}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x| & =t+K \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|x| & =3t+3K \\\\ x & ={e}^{3t+3K} \\\\ x & =C{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=2$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=2,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)=2{e}^{3t}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256606 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bd220d6-2d57-5a20-9815-41cc1315dfc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{v}^{2})=3{e}^{x}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253762} {:answer/id #uuid "278a18b4-76f4-5e4b-9a16-d80569c58fea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=3{e}^{x}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254021} {:answer/id #uuid "2b60c09e-22e6-5285-b087-6989d01f0023" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=3{e}^{x}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254056} {:answer/id #uuid "391f4af1-1c5a-5f46-ba26-d2d5f64442b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=3{e}^{x}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254167} {:answer/id #uuid "eff951cd-1bad-5ebc-a893-9192385cbff4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({v}^{2})=3{e}^{x}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255769}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "391f4af1-1c5a-5f46-ba26-d2d5f64442b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=3{e}^{x}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254167} :answer-field/id #uuid "420d9ebd-fd22-53d2-b5da-7a6057798e29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256045}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "87e41470-a296-5303-9038-b8074f8d3617" :question/math-academy-id "q-122315" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is $(3{e}^{x}+1)\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ Which differential equation describes this motion?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/78/785776cee4f82c5bfa5e86b5b1cb746b7c80f2d702fb52f67055a60bdff249ae.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(3{e}^{x}+1)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=3{e}^{x}+1.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule for differentiation, we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=3{e}^{x}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\nv\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=3{e}^{x}+1.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nFinally, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({v}^{2})=2v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x},\n$$\n\nwe can write our differential equation $(∗)$ as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=3{e}^{x}+1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256607 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7edc5fc6-dbd2-5fb3-b97c-c531b814f79d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\sqrt[3]{(t+1)^{2}}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254793} {:answer/id #uuid "8055693e-68aa-5594-9993-562d5d694b91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\sqrt[3]{1-t}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254804} {:answer/id #uuid "834a1e0b-0234-58e8-bf37-c88100050100" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\sqrt[3]{3t-1}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254838} {:answer/id #uuid "a65db2d2-b201-5520-be67-f1d8406dfee5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=2\\sqrt[3]{3t+1}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255135} {:answer/id #uuid "af13a65e-b324-55aa-b030-c1661cea9d14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\sqrt[3]{3t+1}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255209}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af13a65e-b324-55aa-b030-c1661cea9d14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\sqrt[3]{3t+1}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255209} :answer-field/id #uuid "25a577ca-2103-5969-80ef-827785195e68" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255986}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f9f18290-2aa5-5022-aced-e9bd029cf4d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-122316" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x\\ge 0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, the velocity is given by $v(x)=\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}}\\,m/s.$ Initially, $P$ is at the origin $O.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/20/2093298b6cacb0b29f874d401ccca40560517ff5887763e9a2b88bf4bc051abb.png)\n\nWe're given that the velocity function $v(x)$ is\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}} \\\\ (1+x)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1 \\\\ \\int (1+x)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int (1+x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}(1+x)^{3} & =t+K \\\\ (1+x)^{3} & =3t+C \\\\ 1+x & =\\sqrt[3]{3t+C} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt[3]{3t+C}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=1,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\sqrt[3]{3t+1}-1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256608 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a314d43-645b-5c09-84cc-babb825962d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({v}^{3})=2{x}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253749} {:answer/id #uuid "399960c6-8915-55cf-b499-430d9554395e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=2{x}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254174} {:answer/id #uuid "ab9f49c1-1e43-5f1b-aef8-2b18efec441e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{v}^{2})=2{x}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255187} {:answer/id #uuid "c08c3b51-5261-57f3-8880-d02902e00fe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{4}{v}^{2})=2{x}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255385} {:answer/id #uuid "cbeb2a0e-c3b9-59b0-8c51-5cca3ce4d472" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{3}{v}^{3})=2{x}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255481}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "399960c6-8915-55cf-b499-430d9554395e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=2{x}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254174} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae42a556-6e64-58a1-9826-bc4a1d962a7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256237}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3f3eb3e3-8dee-5051-9c40-8fe59ac84fa9" :question/math-academy-id "q-122319" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is $2{x}^{4}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ Which differential equation describes this motion?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/b2/b23ef6e4df05874a715271c578dfa57d5ddacabef2d0801edc4e736c7de66e17.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $2{x}^{4}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=2{x}^{4}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule for differentiation, we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=2{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\nv\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=2{x}^{4}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nFinally, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({v}^{2})=2v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x},\n$$\n\nwe can write our differential equation $(∗)$ as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=2{x}^{4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256609 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d63a7eb-e93b-56ae-aaae-bd2c21810fe7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=2t{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254075} {:answer/id #uuid "a71abdea-8037-51da-a4db-5998e292e194" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=2-2{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255144} {:answer/id #uuid "d0b0bbda-e1e8-570c-8101-087d5d63fd17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=1-{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255530} {:answer/id #uuid "e43cf2ea-d08d-5829-886b-92dbbe348c60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=2{e}^{2t}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255690} {:answer/id #uuid "fc8ad567-1671-5fab-b7a0-56097e2a4ce8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={e}^{-2t}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255889}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a71abdea-8037-51da-a4db-5998e292e194" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=2-2{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255144} :answer-field/id #uuid "10068344-2ee3-5022-b624-da42939688bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255941}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "dc4b9dea-66c3-5a63-94d7-469085f121c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-122321" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, the velocity is given by $v(x)=(4-2x)\\,m/s.$ Initially, $P$ is at the origin $O.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/7b/7bd0200a651aec7283e5c96d3cdc1589d795531e179ca0eb7cc36ef5bad8de56.png)\n\nWe're given that the velocity function $v(x)$ is\n\n$$\nv(x)=4-2x.\n$$\n\nWe know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=4-2x.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =4-2x \\\\ \\frac{1}{4-2x}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{4-2x}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{4-2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2-x| & =t+K \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|2-x| & =-2t-2K \\\\ 2-x & =C{e}^{-2t} \\\\ x & =2-C{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=2,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)=2-2{e}^{-2t}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256610 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36fb7b17-2720-5311-bf62-05ebd65fac20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254144} {:answer/id #uuid "55a5a2ba-d219-5970-be84-fc13b12d35b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254418} {:answer/id #uuid "8f9e88f6-157f-5107-b46d-3d2331ce92b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254928} {:answer/id #uuid "9e0857c4-4213-574e-bce0-f88b792fccce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255061} {:answer/id #uuid "df9fa467-35c4-5151-8e7f-9e6d7a2baa32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4t+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255656}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36fb7b17-2720-5311-bf62-05ebd65fac20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254144} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3afbccc-6833-56cf-893c-940dbed04af2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256353}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "369e6f0f-7047-5c55-94d8-f747757e554c" :question/math-academy-id "q-122325" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, the velocity is given by $v(x)={e}^{-2x}\\,m/s.$ Initially, $P$ is at the origin $O.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/8f/8faba5a783797f7025f51ec1ae587cb68355dff7138278689346c2f713275562.png)\n\nWe're given that the velocity function $v(x)$ is\n\n$$\nv(x)={e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nWe know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & ={e}^{-2x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{e}^{-2x}}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1 \\\\ \\int {e}^{2x}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x} & =t+K \\\\ {e}^{2x} & =2t+C \\\\ 2x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+C) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=1,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256612 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "428601f8-f190-599b-afaf-931044a1f6bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=\\sqrt{{e}^{x-3}+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254238} {:answer/id #uuid "4f5a0afd-b185-55d5-afe7-e5d4fa397b25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=\\sqrt{3{e}^{x-3}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254360} {:answer/id #uuid "9b583208-ef05-52b8-a320-4f2a849c50a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=3{e}^{x-3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255039} {:answer/id #uuid "c9ef33dc-efa0-518b-af08-5d66f9582ea0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)={e}^{x-3}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255464} {:answer/id #uuid "d9653f61-b17f-5650-9195-db83bb981001" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=\\sqrt{2({e}^{x-3}+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255596}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9653f61-b17f-5650-9195-db83bb981001" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=\\sqrt{2({e}^{x-3}+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255596} :answer-field/id #uuid "80aefb3e-5bf8-5fba-acb9-0aae0cdfacf1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256163}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f89a4daf-6ee5-589b-bab1-0cefd5941b63" :question/math-academy-id "q-122337" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is ${e}^{x-3}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $P$ has a displacement of $3\\,m$ when its velocity is $2\\,m/s,$ find $v$ in terms of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/16/164c90893a07ae9657e90b6f2b25e53c9c4927b1f4a076429ba0521a98377f24.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is ${e}^{x-3}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{x-3}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})={e}^{x-3}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int {e}^{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}={e}^{x-3}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $x=3\\,\\text{m}$ when $v=2\\,\\text{m/s},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={e}^{x-3}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(2)^{2} & ={e}^{3-3}+C \\\\ 2 & =1+C \\\\ C & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={e}^{x-3}+1 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =2({e}^{x-3}+1) \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{2({e}^{x-3}+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\sqrt{2({e}^{x-3}+1)}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256613 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "016a3368-f9d5-58aa-ab05-aad63edb1b18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)={x}^{2}+x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253680} {:answer/id #uuid "1e97ff28-e0bb-54b7-b8b3-9417ec6fd261" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=\\sqrt{2({x}^{2}+x+6)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253929} {:answer/id #uuid "2371f7e7-c2e4-57e1-bd77-d71c072ed039" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+x+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253988} {:answer/id #uuid "bb5e00e1-832f-5f55-83f9-b8c48153a1c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=\\frac{4}{3}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+2x+6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255339} {:answer/id #uuid "fbf76d94-8303-5033-a055-32483b709927" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)={x}^{2}+3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255884}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e97ff28-e0bb-54b7-b8b3-9417ec6fd261" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=\\sqrt{2({x}^{2}+x+6)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253929} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a51decc-a151-5408-9864-1176626e3665" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256148}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4bd8ece4-afaf-5640-9ad2-c2e8ef66ae37" :question/math-academy-id "q-122338" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is $(2x+1)\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $P$ has a displacement of $1\\,m$ when its velocity is $4\\,m/s,$ find $v$ in terms of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/98/983beaf02582c5ebaee129e28bba657eb2db5e4da7f74eb4b8eba73b9083a259.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(2x+1)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=2x+1.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=2x+1.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}={x}^{2}+x+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $x=1\\,\\text{m}$ when $v=4\\,\\text{m/s},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+x+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} & ={1}^{2}+1+C \\\\ 8 & =2+C \\\\ C & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+x+6 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =2({x}^{2}+x+6) \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{2({x}^{2}+x+6)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\sqrt{2({x}^{2}+x+6)}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256614 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fbf0b1a-11d8-5e2c-92ac-c277ea57cf0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=-\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253946} {:answer/id #uuid "612bb86a-2dfd-568a-8b34-e3142b3cc7aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254540} {:answer/id #uuid "ad02467e-e19b-56c4-b11f-a03290a66053" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{64-{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255195} {:answer/id #uuid "c5c3a766-1c93-55cd-8d1b-0364c1f33dca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255439} {:answer/id #uuid "eb1be328-ccab-5215-adf7-480720d3e81e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=-\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255727}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1fbf0b1a-11d8-5e2c-92ac-c277ea57cf0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=-\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253946} :answer-field/id #uuid "12b06d80-5c3b-5c12-a9b5-466da7006a82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255948}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "213f127e-e7f0-5df3-8b8e-59dbe65f8117" :question/math-academy-id "q-122339" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of decreasing $x$ where $x>0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is $\\frac{1}{16}x\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $P$ has a displacement of $0\\,m$ when its velocity is $-2\\,m/s,$ find $v$ in terms of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/ba/ba6a85da04d9ba03bcdba9b938dac208a770933310cd25c19bf0ec3e19ef17c7.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $\\frac{1}{16}x\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{16}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=\\frac{1}{16}x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (\\frac{1}{16}x)\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=\\frac{1}{32}{x}^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $x=0\\,\\text{m}$ when $v=-2\\,\\text{m/s},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{32}{x}^{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(-2)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{32}(0)^{2}+C \\\\ 2 & =0+C \\\\ C & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{32}{x}^{2}+2 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{16}{x}^{2}+4 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{16}({x}^{2}+64) \\\\ v & =\\pm \\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+64}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)<0,$ so we can discard the positive square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=-\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+64}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256615 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "372a2bdf-ee81-5097-8dbb-fd3dc0fa42e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=-2\\sqrt{2(x+{x}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254147} {:answer/id #uuid "3c6c2342-4e07-5b82-ad5a-87d2e863c563" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=-2(x+{x}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254195} {:answer/id #uuid "53ec6da2-ab5c-5631-be67-3eafdf3b3996" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=-(2+x+{x}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254403} {:answer/id #uuid "78d2817b-de64-5b90-ada6-b3887e34e5f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=-\\sqrt{2(3x+5{x}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254738} {:answer/id #uuid "c008b9a2-7e53-56f3-a280-8d93c778cb4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=-2\\sqrt{3x+{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255378}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "372a2bdf-ee81-5097-8dbb-fd3dc0fa42e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=-2\\sqrt{2(x+{x}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254147} :answer-field/id #uuid "466ccb33-63bb-57d2-be5f-5a1d192507f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256055}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "888253db-091b-5a6a-99ae-2649a14413e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-122340" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of decreasing $x$ where $x>0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is $4(1+2x)\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $P$ has a displacement of $1\\,m$ when its velocity is $-4\\,m/s,$ find $v$ in terms of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/46/466c0aa389beb6d1a42092b73bae75508ef2c50f1f08cca5efaac20d25615f30.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $4(1+2x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=4(1+2x).\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=4(1+2x).\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int 4(1+2x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=4(x+{x}^{2})+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $x=1\\,\\text{m}$ when $v=-4\\,\\text{m/s},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =4(x+{x}^{2})+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(-4)^{2} & =4(1+{1}^{2})+C \\\\ 8 & =8+C \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =4(x+{x}^{2})+0 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =8(x+{x}^{2}) \\\\ v & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{2(x+{x}^{2})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)<0,$ so we can discard the positive square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=-2\\sqrt{2(x+{x}^{2})}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256616 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55e5f7fd-5f39-54d0-b24b-d6386ba4b120" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)={2}^{3-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254422} {:answer/id #uuid "7715943a-4b29-5731-aa1b-21b6a9f5bcda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)={2}^{1-x}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254725} {:answer/id #uuid "7a7f8494-68d3-5ece-b55e-5a21d83663af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=2\\sqrt{3\\cdot {2}^{1-x}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254759} {:answer/id #uuid "bbaa6296-4ac5-5e74-907b-e485c987c549" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=2\\sqrt{{2}^{1-x}+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255345} {:answer/id #uuid "cfa68c5c-78bd-533d-9a2b-0493c8ac2f7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=\\frac{4}{3}\\sqrt{{2}^{3-x}+5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255520}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bbaa6296-4ac5-5e74-907b-e485c987c549" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=2\\sqrt{{2}^{1-x}+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255345} :answer-field/id #uuid "6837870e-c4ce-53d3-8d92-89b9b7c8217b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256116}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9efcc68b-3929-5bf9-ab56-4c19b9a57740" :question/math-academy-id "q-122341" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x>0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ the magnitude of its acceleration is ${2}^{2-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and the acceleration is directed toward $O.$ If $P$ has a displacement of $1\\,m$ when its velocity is $4\\,m/s,$ find $v$ in terms of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/6d/6ddbfef68a0b41a6ee3f11c46ff7821f7ff0dc23a16b4244b5e7a1f6579d7dd2.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude ${2}^{2-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-{2}^{2-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-{2}^{2-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (-{2}^{2-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}={2}^{2-x}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $x=1\\,\\text{m}$ when $v=4\\,\\text{m/s},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={2}^{2-x}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} & ={2}^{2-1}+C \\\\ 8 & =2+C \\\\ C & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={2}^{2-x}+6 \\\\ {v}^{2} & ={2}^{3-x}+12 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =4({2}^{1-x}+3) \\\\ v & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{{2}^{1-x}+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=2\\sqrt{{2}^{1-x}+3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256617 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "032c1aed-5869-5996-b874-ec60376b4b7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.8\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253693} {:answer/id #uuid "3c64d957-8cab-5c68-a933-ebb3da3c2a75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254193} {:answer/id #uuid "484acfee-7a2b-52bf-a78b-d62f355b27df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.3\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254288} {:answer/id #uuid "caa5dc97-9ff5-56d3-816d-361c1ad73349" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.3\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255472} {:answer/id #uuid "d91174a7-6095-546d-814c-2cd0e4c8e550" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.6\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255593}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "032c1aed-5869-5996-b874-ec60376b4b7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.8\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253693} :answer-field/id #uuid "0641e0bb-3057-51af-9319-471e1e0b8f5b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255926}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8a56f0b7-8350-56d0-8d3b-b2d932d38f5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-122343" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x,$ away from a fixed origin $O.$ When the displacement of $P$ from $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is ${e}^{x/2}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $v=\\sqrt{5}\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,\\text{m},$ find the velocity of $P$ when $x=1\\,\\text{m}.$ Round your answer to one decimal place." :question/worked-solution "![](images/0d/0dd98b517e2ed800f12a3672d2d6f45021f7f502cfe98c1e9308e52c05ab5f38.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is ${e}^{x/2}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{x/2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})={e}^{x/2}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{x/2}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=2{e}^{x/2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since  $v=\\sqrt{5}\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,\\text{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{5})^{2} & =2{e}^{0/2}+C \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} & =2+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =2{e}^{x/2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =4{e}^{x/2}+1 \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{4{e}^{x/2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv=\\sqrt{4{e}^{x/2}+1}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity of $P$ at $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\sqrt{4{e}^{1/2}+1} \\\\ v & =\\sqrt{4\\sqrt{e}+1} \\\\  & ≈2.8\\,\\text{m/s},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256618 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26e4a0c4-6748-530e-b1bf-2353a84d0940" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{{t}^{2}}{1-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254014} {:answer/id #uuid "55fa3efb-4486-5701-ab33-e2e05b4074c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=2t-{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254424} {:answer/id #uuid "6bdfb113-3a39-5ab7-8a51-1a5e2943e267" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{t}{1-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254629} {:answer/id #uuid "d067e231-e8f6-56b8-ae25-58b9bb109978" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=t-4{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255527} {:answer/id #uuid "ecc5b195-cc1e-5e84-abbf-fc2b6bc7692f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{4t}{1-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255737}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ecc5b195-cc1e-5e84-abbf-fc2b6bc7692f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{4t}{1-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255737} :answer-field/id #uuid "92693160-7882-5164-942f-40cb3e461559" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256188}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bb122141-01a3-58a4-8e6a-d0821d276131" :question/math-academy-id "q-122344" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,\\text{m},$ its acceleration is $2(x+2)^{3}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ Initially, $P$ is at the origin $O$ and has velocity $v=4\\,m/s.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t$ where $t\\in [0,\\frac{1}{4}].$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/ba/ba9988a12d1c70c43a6a010c3578850fe79a9bca01309884fe981b3f7cca512c.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $2(x+2)^{3}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=2(x+2)^{3}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=2(x+2)^{3}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2(x+2)^{3}\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+2)^{4}+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=4\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $x=0\\,\\text{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $K,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+2)^{4}+K \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(0+2)^{4}+K \\\\ 8 & =8+K \\\\ K & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+2)^{4} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =(x+2)^{4} \\\\ v & =\\pm (x+2)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=(x+2)^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=(x+2)^{2}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(x+2)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int 1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x+2} & =t+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{x+2} & =-t-C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=-\\frac{1}{2},$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{x+2}=-t+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, writing $x$ in terms of $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x+2} & =-t+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x+2} & =\\frac{1-2t}{2} \\\\ x+2 & =\\frac{2}{1-2t} \\\\ x & =\\frac{2}{1-2t}-2 \\\\ x & =\\frac{4t}{1-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256619 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "209e45b5-1f8f-56c6-8da2-50e55b118dea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=(t+2)^{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253956} {:answer/id #uuid "58afcb63-40b0-5ba4-9d7f-fb7f0b8493e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=(t+1)^{3}+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254449} {:answer/id #uuid "912a8c7f-76ca-5201-a60f-8779c2ce1245" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=9-\\sqrt[3]{t+8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254944} {:answer/id #uuid "917afec5-1599-5218-9fe7-f24551713fd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=2(t+2)^{3}-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254948} {:answer/id #uuid "c0109bb6-c067-5da0-8664-b2245b245d71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\sqrt[3]{t+8}+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255379}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "209e45b5-1f8f-56c6-8da2-50e55b118dea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=(t+2)^{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253956} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd010bed-2ba1-50e1-9e58-7a0dd3de6a81" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256290}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b28c1ae8-cb8b-5fb8-b6bd-e337b0efbc6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-122345" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,\\text{m},$ its acceleration is $6\\sqrt[3]{x+1}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ Initially, $P$ is at $x=7\\,m$ and has velocity $v=12\\,m/s.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/63/634a0680d0f159006c524b8d0eb466b2c99b0f9b5a46cf38b7cd689c9d8d6fb2.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $6\\sqrt[3]{x+1}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=6\\sqrt[3]{x+1}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=6\\sqrt[3]{x+1}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 6\\sqrt[3]{x+1}\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{9(x+1)^{4/3}}{2}+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=12\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $x=7\\,\\text{m}$ we can find the value of the constant $K,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{9(x+1)^{4/3}}{2}+K \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(12)^{2} & =\\frac{9(7+1)^{4/3}}{2}+K \\\\ 72 & =72+K \\\\ K & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{9(x+1)^{4/3}}{2} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =9(x+1)^{4/3} \\\\ v & =\\pm 3(x+1)^{2/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=3(x+1)^{2/3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=3(x+1)^{2/3}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =3(x+1)^{2/3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3(x+1)^{2/3}}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{3(x+1)^{2/3}}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{3(x+1)^{2/3}}\\text{d}x & =\\int \\text{d}t \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{x+1} & =t+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=7$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=2,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{x+1}=t+2.\n$$\n\nFinally, writing $x$ in terms of $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{x+1} & =t+2 \\\\ x+1 & =(t+2)^{3} \\\\ x & =(t+2)^{3}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256620 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c7a03e2-f7fc-5c8a-809b-37a3a1c6e673" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.8\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253771} {:answer/id #uuid "12c55bbc-c7d5-5426-91b8-d47869b42dbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.5\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253834} {:answer/id #uuid "9e704051-433d-5b1f-af7b-cb3d5cd99ddb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.1\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255066} {:answer/id #uuid "b7b417a2-af06-50cc-b358-bccec8f94e7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.9\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255302} {:answer/id #uuid "f0d07714-f0dc-5d23-99dd-970ad81c7eb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.3\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255774}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7b417a2-af06-50cc-b358-bccec8f94e7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.9\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255302} :answer-field/id #uuid "da27d9e7-75e0-5068-a453-a03455592f0b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256308}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e24e75b5-6a79-5c50-9102-1dd282764c54" :question/math-academy-id "q-122346" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x,$ away from a fixed origin $O.$ When the displacement of $P$ from $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is $(2+\\frac{x}{2}){m/s}^{2}.$ If $v=5\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,\\text{m},$ find the velocity of $P$ when $x=2\\,\\text{m}.$ Round your answer to one decimal place." :question/worked-solution "![](images/d9/d9482b116bad201dec2c71f173bf18d7dc2f170acbb37052151bba38a7898450.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(2+\\frac{x}{2}){m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=2+\\frac{x}{2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=2+\\frac{x}{2}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x & =\\int (2+\\frac{x}{2})\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=2x+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=5\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,\\text{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(5)^{2} & =2(0)+\\frac{{0}^{2}}{4}+C \\\\ \\frac{25}{2} & =0+0+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{25}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =2x+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{25}{2} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+4x+25 \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+4x+25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv=\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+4x+25}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity of $P$ at $x=2$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}+4(2)+25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2+8+25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{35} \\\\  & ≈5.9\\,\\text{m/s},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256621 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8658c0bb-cfd4-5142-bbcb-e4097359b029" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3.7\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254860} {:answer/id #uuid "9dc47ff9-abd8-5a14-858a-de531e040383" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.9\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255056} {:answer/id #uuid "b5ad6d9c-592b-5e0c-8122-d62c500c3d21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.8\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255273} {:answer/id #uuid "b9989186-dace-584b-b203-97a637274263" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3.1\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255322} {:answer/id #uuid "dd0cc1f7-7a63-5b1c-a0d5-346590852e30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.3\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255630}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9989186-dace-584b-b203-97a637274263" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3.1\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255322} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcd66982-5b26-55ef-acff-14d539285859" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256362}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8d544f0e-e224-5a8f-b2d9-7d23deb9a689" :question/math-academy-id "q-122347" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of decreasing $x$ where $x>0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ the magnitude of its acceleration is ${3}^{1-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and the acceleration is directed toward $O.$ If $v=-2\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s$ when $x=2\\,\\text{m},$ find the velocity of $P$ when $x=1\\,\\text{m}.$ Round your answer to one decimal place." :question/worked-solution "![](images/3c/3c5450b18c5dc58b082faadf4086b6a21c0c9b55afb016090147609a2ccfe719.png)\n\nSince the magnitude of the acceleration of the particle $P$ is ${3}^{1-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-{3}^{1-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-{3}^{1-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x & =\\int [-{3}^{1-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3]\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}={3}^{1-x}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=-2\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s$ when $x=2\\,\\text{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(-2\\sqrt{2})^{2} & ={3}^{1-2}+C \\\\ 4 & =\\frac{1}{3}+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{11}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={3}^{1-x}+\\frac{11}{3} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =2({3}^{1-x}+\\frac{11}{3}) \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{2({3}^{1-x}+\\frac{11}{3})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)<0,$ so we can discard the positive square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv=-\\sqrt{2({3}^{1-x}+\\frac{11}{3})}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity of $P$ at $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =-\\sqrt{2({3}^{1-1}+\\frac{11}{3})} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{\\frac{28}{3}} \\\\  & ≈-3.1\\,\\text{m/s},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256622 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43a37d05-93cf-5d34-976f-81987d20a896" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.9\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254247} {:answer/id #uuid "53e374ac-50ea-5858-84fb-e361a9850e25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3.1\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254401} {:answer/id #uuid "5ea0cc67-272f-5bd1-97cc-b3ff160fe065" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.1\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254514} {:answer/id #uuid "91f4e784-aae3-566d-9825-bc8ecd6f410b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.6\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254950} {:answer/id #uuid "b4314fca-414f-5aaa-ade9-d4f42fd6b5e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.7\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255258}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ea0cc67-272f-5bd1-97cc-b3ff160fe065" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.1\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254514} :answer-field/id #uuid "1685d0b0-562f-56c8-a207-4572c25c4216" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255959}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ac224c51-f1a6-5430-b1c6-c86ec1a024d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-122348" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of decreasing $x$ where $x>0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ the magnitude of its acceleration is $\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and the acceleration is directed toward $O.$ If $v=-\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,m/s$ when $x=1\\,\\text{m},$ find the velocity of $P$ when $x=3\\,\\text{m}.$ Round your answer to one decimal place." :question/worked-solution "![](images/32/3278eca2ae91f59c7c20d91f9190611291924dcc0cd6ffe74456552ba8400335.png)\n\nSince the magnitude of the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+1}\\right)\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=-2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=-\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,m/s$ when $x=1\\,\\text{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(-\\sqrt{\\pi })^{2} & =-2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1+C \\\\ \\frac{\\pi }{2} & =-2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}+C \\\\ C & =\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\pi -2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x \\\\ {v}^{2} & =2(\\pi -2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x) \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{2(\\pi -2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)<0,$ so we can discard the positive square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv=-\\sqrt{2(\\pi -2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity of $P$ at $x=3$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =-\\sqrt{2(\\pi -2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡3)} \\\\  & ≈-1.1\\,\\text{m/s},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256623 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4364ff09-2922-52f1-bb5f-e67a3ba34a6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\sqrt[3]{12t+8}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254246} {:answer/id #uuid "5b2164e7-b48b-5848-83ea-558ab8d57139" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\sqrt[3]{2t+1}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254476} {:answer/id #uuid "5cc028c3-519a-5570-a621-cd76222813f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=(12t-1)^{3}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254492} {:answer/id #uuid "da22387e-a7bf-5ddb-9d10-96871c121ede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\sqrt[3]{t-8}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255605} {:answer/id #uuid "fd4a3bfe-c5a0-5f5f-b52c-5d5d734cceb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=(3t+2)^{3}-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255897}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4364ff09-2922-52f1-bb5f-e67a3ba34a6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\sqrt[3]{12t+8}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254246} :answer-field/id #uuid "f893dcb4-30ee-50df-9369-932bd543c3cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256358}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ec791f70-e6b1-5d28-9a5c-0e20d98d3307" :question/math-academy-id "q-122349" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x\\ge 0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,\\text{m},$ the magnitude of its acceleration is $\\frac{32}{(x+2)^{5}}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed toward $O.$ Initially, $P$ is at the origin $O$ and has velocity $v=1\\,m/s.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/14/141c6dd6c91841238298b86fac1063c76faeb7cd103e13d2723f6ac05ed58f50.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $\\frac{32}{(x+2)^{5}}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{32}{(x+2)^{5}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-\\frac{32}{(x+2)^{5}}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -\\frac{32}{(x+2)^{5}}\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{8}{(x+2)^{4}}+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=1\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $x=0\\,\\text{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $K,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{8}{(x+2)^{4}}+K \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(1)^{2} & =\\frac{8}{(0+2)^{4}}+K \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}+K \\\\ K & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{8}{(x+2)^{4}} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =\\frac{16}{(x+2)^{4}} \\\\ v & =\\pm \\frac{4}{(x+2)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\frac{4}{(x+2)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNow, we know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{4}{(x+2)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{4}{(x+2)^{2}} \\\\ (x+2)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =4 \\\\ \\int (x+2)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int (x+2)^{2}\\text{d}x & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}(x+2)^{3} & =4t+{C}_{1} \\\\ (x+2)^{3} & =12t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=3{C}_{1}.$ \n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=8,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\n(x+2)^{3}=12t+8.\n$$\n\nFinally, writing $x$ in terms of $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)^{3} & =12t+8 \\\\ x+2 & =\\sqrt[3]{12t+8} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt[3]{12t+8}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256625 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39825938-ad5f-5161-82be-a58bd1817a1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254173} {:answer/id #uuid "8ee5c0c6-750e-5668-bdc0-064e5c9b4f07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1)-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254925} {:answer/id #uuid "a5170868-e27c-5ec2-bb54-083bfe57da02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=2{e}^{-2t}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255126} {:answer/id #uuid "dcd96b2d-0804-5239-be68-eadc7bae04bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255629} {:answer/id #uuid "e85d341b-3675-5698-9153-635f1bc811e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255710}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dcd96b2d-0804-5239-be68-eadc7bae04bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255629} :answer-field/id #uuid "795c6c90-3b12-5b40-b826-7991e61fdee3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256144}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ac389cb5-50b0-5d9d-9387-c7be6e7524a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-122352" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x\\ge 0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,\\text{m},$ the magnitude of its acceleration is $8{e}^{1-x}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed toward $O.$ Initially, $P$ is at $x=1\\,m$ and has velocity $v=4\\,m/s.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/f0/f08c9d1e8dce3c0ae7c37719cadc3fc60bf5de3030ce8c44ca7528a98f3abf7e.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $8{e}^{1-x}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-8{e}^{1-x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-8{e}^{1-x}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -8{e}^{1-x}\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =8{e}^{1-x}+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=4\\,m/s$ when $x=1\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $K,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =8{e}^{1-x}+K \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} & =8{e}^{0}+K \\\\ 8 & =8+K \\\\ K & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =8{e}^{1-x} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =16{e}^{1-x} \\\\ v & =\\pm 4{e}^{(1-x)/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=4{e}^{(1-x)/2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=4{e}^{(1-x)/2}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =4{e}^{(1-x)/2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{e}^{(1-x)/2}}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =4 \\\\ \\int {e}^{(x-1)/2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int {e}^{(x-1)/2}\\text{d}x & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ 2{e}^{(x-1)/2} & =4t+{C}_{1} \\\\ {e}^{(x-1)/2} & =2t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=\\frac{{C}_{1}}{2}.$ \n\nWe're given that $x=1$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=1,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{e}^{(x-1)/2}=2t+1.\n$$\n\nFinally, writing $x$ in terms of $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{(x-1)/2} & =2t+1 \\\\ \\frac{x-1}{2} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1) \\\\ x-1 & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1) \\\\ x & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1)+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367191 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "753bc59c-7939-56cc-879b-3cec122d331b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=\\sqrt[3]{3{x}^{2}+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367193} {:answer/id #uuid "f24d7098-d939-5aa6-950f-fd14eb017097" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{v}^{2})=\\sqrt[3]{3{x}^{2}+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367194} {:answer/id #uuid "9bbc9175-4c96-5988-aa07-bd9572c01406" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\sqrt[3]{3{x}^{2}+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367195} {:answer/id #uuid "2d505332-cf6c-5ec5-915e-4c36344f470f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\sqrt[3]{3{x}^{2}+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367196} {:answer/id #uuid "eb2c040f-5718-5092-9cb7-71a8d668b302" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{4}{v}^{2})=\\sqrt[3]{3{x}^{2}+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367197}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "753bc59c-7939-56cc-879b-3cec122d331b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=\\sqrt[3]{3{x}^{2}+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367193} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a99db37-d18c-5eb8-8ecd-26a2b8b0ca2b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367192}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "176edb15-1bd9-590d-8107-8cb1c8611e83" :question/math-academy-id "q-122317" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is $\\sqrt[3]{3{x}^{2}+2}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ Which differential equation describes this motion?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/30/30e0e58a17f1683f1c6ca58ea8e4d3df97d7edb76676b0ed6d1a75f62d0c0803.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $\\sqrt[3]{3{x}^{2}+2}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\sqrt[3]{3{x}^{2}+2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule for differentiation, we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\sqrt[3]{3{x}^{2}+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\nv\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\sqrt[3]{3{x}^{2}+2}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nFinally, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({v}^{2})=2v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x},\n$$\n\nwe can write our differential equation $(∗)$ as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=\\sqrt[3]{3{x}^{2}+2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378470 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b924fff7-e1f3-501e-8490-87fa8faeb24c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378472} {:answer/id #uuid "64de2085-9a68-50d7-bbc4-040c3e8e3a91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378473} {:answer/id #uuid "2c67d96c-5069-5cab-942b-aeb5c32db0a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378474} {:answer/id #uuid "ae9923eb-45e9-595c-a6d7-22103fd07456" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{v}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378475} {:answer/id #uuid "f209f354-0c2c-56dd-8fc2-3a7811b29d24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378476}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c67d96c-5069-5cab-942b-aeb5c32db0a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378474} :answer-field/id #uuid "06ab7111-1425-5b44-aaeb-a47933078f99" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378471}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a41d772e-2bfb-5d10-922c-6b9bac42e653" :question/math-academy-id "q-122304" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\,\\text{m/s}$ and its acceleration is $\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ Which differential equation describes this motion?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/f0/f03ad90557b4bf978ece899892cada200873e920c8e00a0c196728108fee55c4.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule for differentiation, we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we obtain the differential equation\n\n$$\nv\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382971 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f3d3af1d-a407-58f8-aaf2-23b16080ae71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382973} {:answer/id #uuid "54983bd5-fbaa-56d1-8f98-0e4eecb21269" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382974} {:answer/id #uuid "9be55074-f649-5696-b220-cabe8539f6f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382975} {:answer/id #uuid "9d131aee-ffab-5b37-847e-ea2f674267b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382976} {:answer/id #uuid "a5b213ee-36ed-5ee8-a20e-ff7f9e72b9e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382977}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54983bd5-fbaa-56d1-8f98-0e4eecb21269" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382974} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac7ed411-8375-5b91-ac3e-342c110e5952" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382972}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7832c005-e5d4-5bbe-91b5-55956ce35ed8" :question/math-academy-id "q-122342" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x,$ away from a fixed origin $O.$ When the displacement of $P$ from $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is $(4{x}^{3}+4x)\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $v=\\sqrt{6}\\,m/s$ when $x=1\\,\\text{m},$ find the velocity of $P$ when $x=2\\,\\text{m}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/4e/4e0b8b816ca3b86fa90ea785df1308f53a4763baa2c2aa2192b9d7bfa0259855.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(4{x}^{3}+4x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=4{x}^{3}+4x.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=4{x}^{3}+4x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x & =\\int (4{x}^{3}+4x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}={x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\sqrt{6}\\,m/s$ when $x=1\\,\\text{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{6})^{2} & ={1}^{4}+2(1)^{2}+C \\\\ 3 & =3+C \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={x}^{4}+2{x}^{2} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =2({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}) \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{2({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv=\\sqrt{2({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2})}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity of $P$ at $x=2$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\sqrt{2({2}^{4}+2(2)^{2})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{48} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{m/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400058 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb841386-1180-59be-8b8f-ef4a3bdf4bae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=(3t+1)^{4}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400060} {:answer/id #uuid "e0471f0f-25c1-5626-b98b-3b2fe326dc7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=(3t+1)^{4/3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400061} {:answer/id #uuid "6ed50d71-47ef-504d-b078-392fbb23bb85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=(4t+1)^{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400062} {:answer/id #uuid "303677bb-cada-51a9-b7bf-74bbee38a830" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=(t+2)^{2/3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400063} {:answer/id #uuid "2ddf2ba5-5cf7-5f47-befa-2cacf9a12596" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=(t+2)^{4/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400064}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0471f0f-25c1-5626-b98b-3b2fe326dc7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=(3t+1)^{4/3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400061} :answer-field/id #uuid "56af02a6-0883-5dcb-9c23-0114fa511fa6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400059}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "528f1204-b150-5ae3-b3f2-1df94a35f90d" :question/math-academy-id "q-122351" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,\\text{m},$ its acceleration is $\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x+1}}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ Initially, $P$ is at the origin $O$ and has velocity $v=4\\,m/s.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/ee/ee516744d915553bf962b498af522cf0006d42c5d0e0e2a003e6f385dd069e4a.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x+1}}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x+1}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x+1}}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x+1}}\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =8\\sqrt{x+1}+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $x=0\\,\\text{m}$ when $v=4\\,\\text{m/s},$ we can find the value of the constant $K,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =8\\sqrt{x+1}+K \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} & =8\\sqrt{0+1}+K \\\\ 8 & =8+K \\\\ K & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =8\\sqrt{x+1} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =16\\sqrt{x+1} \\\\ v & =\\pm 4\\sqrt[4]{x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=4\\sqrt[4]{x+1}.\n$$\n\nNow, we know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=4\\sqrt[4]{x+1}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\sqrt[4]{x+1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[4]{x+1}}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =4 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[4]{x+1}}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}(x+1)^{3/4} & =4t+{C}_{1} \\\\ (x+1)^{3/4} & =3t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=\\frac{3{C}_{1}}{4}.$ \n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=1,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\n(x+1)^{3/4}=3t+1.\n$$\n\nFinally, writing $x$ in terms of $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{3/4} & =3t+1 \\\\ x+1 & =(3t+1)^{4/3} \\\\ x & =(3t+1)^{4/3}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
